e first big slide. The engine preferred G6. The wall was growing, but it was growing along White's stones on row 7."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The 20k's B4 lost 6.2 points, its worst move, and the lead went from -10.0 to -3.8. The engine wanted B3. Black had the chance to get back into the game here."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "D3 lost 3.8 points, and the lead went from -3.8 to -7.6. The engine's choice was B3, the very point that would have punished B4, so Black gave back most of what the 20k had just offered."}, {"turn": 25, "text": "G4 was Black's worst move, losing 5.9 points and taking the lead from -11.9 to -17.9. The engine preferred J4. The 20k answered J6, which lost 5.8 itself, but Black was still far behind."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "A4 lost 4.5 points, though the lead stayed at -9.9, and the 20k now held the left edge from A4 to C4 above Black's row-3 stones. Black spent the rest of the game on the lower right, but the points were already on White's side."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran works on the current position or on records: the stored `inspect` function lists existing chains and liberties and the effect of one named move (C3, G3, etc.), `parse` reads the board text, and the region count at move 41 flood-fills empty areas. The rest is replay of the actual move list, SGF writing, and note files; nothing loops over candidate moves or looks ahead.", "stop": false}}